CC0 1.0 Universal — Public Domain Dedication


WHAT THIS COVERS

Everything in this repository:

  * protocol definitions (the *.json files)
  * sample captures committed alongside them (*.sub, *.cu8 and any other recording)
  * the documentation in this repository

It does NOT cover the Signal Visor application itself, which is closed source and distributed
separately, and it does NOT cover outside recording collections (the rtl_433 test suite, the
Flipper Zero collection), which the application's tests refer to but which are never copied into
this repository.


THE DEDICATION

To the extent possible under law, the contributors have waived all copyright and related or
neighbouring rights to the material described above. The work is dedicated to the public domain
worldwide.

You may copy, modify, distribute and use the material, including for commercial purposes, all
without asking permission and without attribution of any kind.

The full legal text is at: https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode


WHY

The point of a protocol library is that it spreads. A definition is mostly a set of facts about
somebody else's device — a timing, a bit layout, a polynomial — and locking facts away helps no
one. Putting them in the public domain lets rtl_433, ESPHome, Flipper firmware and anyone else
take them directly, which is good for everybody who owns the devices they describe.

Sample captures are under the same dedication, deliberately. A recording is more of a work than
a timing table is, so without a licence nobody may redistribute it, and a test suite that cannot
be redistributed is of no use to anyone else. The external rtl_433 corpus shows the problem: it
is excellent material that declares no licence, so this project can reference it but never ship
it.


CONTRIBUTING

By contributing a definition or a capture you place it under this same dedication. Please only
contribute recordings of your own devices.

No warranty is given. A definition may be wrong, and a device of the same model may differ.
